---
// What the site records about visits, in plain words. Linked from the footer.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/base';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description="What this site records about visits, when it records a session, and how to opt out.">
	<article class="privacy">
		<h1>Privacy</h1>
		<p class="dek">Short version: I see how people find and use this site, not who they are.</p>

		<h2>What's recorded</h2>
		<p>
			This site uses PostHog, hosted in the EU, to understand visits. For each visit it records the pages viewed, how you
			arrived (the site that sent you, or a tag in a link I shared), clicks on the résumé, contact and demo links, your
			browser and device type, and an approximate location (country and city) worked out from your IP address.
		</p>
		<p>
			Your IP address is stored with those events. I use it to see which organization's network a visit came from,
			where that's possible, which mostly works for large employers' office networks. I don't try to identify
			individual people.
		</p>
		<p>
			A recording of your visit, meaning what you see, scroll and click, with anything you type hidden. Recording uses
			a PostHog cookie and browser storage. You can opt out below.
		</p>

		<h2>What isn't</h2>
		<p>
			No advertising trackers, and nothing is sold or shared. If you opt out, there are no cookies or recordings: visits
			are counted with an anonymous hash on PostHog's side, and the only thing kept in your browser is your opt-out.
		</p>

		<h2>Opting out</h2>
		<p>
			If your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, nothing is recorded at all. To have data from your visits deleted, get in touch through the
			<a href={withBase('#contact')}>contact section</a>.
		</p>
		<p>
			<button type="button" data-consent-toggle class="reset">Opt out of cookies and recording</button>
			<span data-consent-status class="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
		</p>

		<h2>Contact details</h2>
		<p>
			To keep my phone number and email away from scrapers, they may appear only after a quick check by Cloudflare
			Turnstile, which looks at browser signals to tell people from bots. That check is covered by Cloudflare's own
			privacy policy.
		</p>

		<h2>Demos</h2>
		<p>The coded-project demos run on their own hosting and don't share data with this site.</p>
	</article>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
	import { getConsent, setConsent } from '../../scripts/analytics';

	// Opt out (or back in). Takes effect from the next page load.
	const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-consent-toggle]');
	const status = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-consent-status]');
	const render = () => {
		const out = getConsent() === 'no';
		if (button) button.textContent = out ? 'Opt back in' : 'Opt out of cookies and recording';
		if (status) status.textContent = out ? ' You’re opted out.' : '';
	};
	render();
	button?.addEventListener('click', () => {
		setConsent(getConsent() === 'no' ? null : 'no');
		render();
	});
</script>

<style>
	.privacy {
		max-width: 44rem;
	}

	.dek {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
		font-size: var(--text-lg);
	}

	.privacy h2 {
		font-size: var(--text-xl);
		margin-top: var(--space-5);
	}

	.reset {
		font: inherit;
		color: var(--color-link);
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-underline-offset: 4px;
		background: none;
		border: 0;
		padding: 0;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	.status {
		color: var(--color-text-muted);
	}

	.privacy p {
		margin: var(--space-3) 0;
		line-height: var(--leading-prose);
	}
</style>
